# Line endings, declared here so they do not depend on anyone's core.autocrlf.
#
# Without this file every clone decides for itself, and this one had drifted
# into three states at once: 414 files checked out CRLF, 197 LF, and
# docs/stellation_app.html carrying seven \r\r\n sequences that no amount of
# committing would clean.
#
# That last one is worth explaining, because it is self-perpetuating and cost
# several confusing sessions. The first \r of a \r\r\n is a LONE CR, and git
# reports a file containing lone CRs as "-text" — not text — which means it
# skips end-of-line normalisation for that file entirely. So the stray CRs
# caused the classification that prevented the stray CRs from being fixed, and
# the file stayed broken through every commit that touched it. Patch scripts
# matching on "\r\n" or "\n" then failed against those lines for no visible
# reason. Removing the lone CRs is what lets git treat the file as text again.
#
# LF in the working tree as well as in the repository. This is a static site
# served straight from the files, edited as often by scripts as by hand, and
# uniform LF means a script never has to guess which ending a file is in —
# guessing wrong is what produced the \r\r\n in the first place.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception: cmd.exe is the one consumer here that has a
# real preference, so they keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Declared rather than sniffed, so a heuristic can never decide otherwise.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.jar binary
*.zip binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
